1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:12,000
At about a quarter past seven on the morning of the thirtieth of June, nineteen-oh-eight, something came out of a clear sky over central Siberia and exploded above the forest.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,199
It flattened more than two thousand square kilometres of taiga.

3
00:00:16,666 --> 00:00:17,983
Investigators came.

4
00:00:17,983 --> 00:00:19,439
They found no crater.

5
00:00:19,439 --> 00:00:22,698
No meteorite was ever pulled out of the ground.

6
00:00:22,698 --> 00:00:29,700
And into that silence moved a famous idea: that this was not a rock at all, but a crashed spacecraft.

7
00:00:30,166 --> 00:00:32,896
A nuclear engine failing over Siberia.

8
00:00:32,896 --> 00:00:35,481
Alien wreckage hidden in the swamps.

9
00:00:35,481 --> 00:00:39,288
Even a ship that sacrificed itself to save the Earth.

10
00:00:39,288 --> 00:00:41,299
You have heard the versions.

11
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:46,256
So tonight we treat Tunguska the way investigators treat a crash.

12
00:00:46,256 --> 00:00:47,292
We open a file.

13
00:00:47,292 --> 00:00:55,166
We take witness statements, survey the site, search for wreckage, rebuild the flight path, and read the recorders.

14
00:00:55,633 --> 00:00:59,533
Then we line up the suspects: comet, asteroid, or craft.

15
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:04,200
And at the end, like any accident board, we state a probable cause.

16
00:01:08,666 --> 00:01:10,793
Section one: the occurrence.

17
00:01:10,793 --> 00:01:19,070
The place is the basin of the Podkamennaya Tunguska river, a tributary of the Yenisei, in the Russian Empire.

18
00:01:19,070 --> 00:01:24,766
Swamp, permafrost and low wooded hills, frozen eight or nine months a year.

19
00:01:25,233 --> 00:01:40,566
The people here were mostly Evenki reindeer herders, living in small family groups in conical tents, and a few Russian traders at posts like Vanavara, about sixty kilometres south of what would become ground zero.

20
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:48,249
That morning, under a clear sky, a fireball brighter than the Sun crossed from the south-east, trailing white.

21
00:01:48,249 --> 00:01:51,333
People saw it from hundreds of kilometres away.

22
00:01:51,799 --> 00:01:55,489
At about seven fourteen local time, it exploded.

23
00:01:55,489 --> 00:02:02,713
A column of fire, a cloud rising tens of kilometres, a heat pulse felt eighty kilometres away.

24
00:02:02,713 --> 00:02:06,633
Windows broke as far as two hundred kilometres out.

25
00:02:07,099 --> 00:02:12,148
At least three people died, all Evenki, and reindeer herds were lost.

26
00:02:12,148 --> 00:02:16,318
It was the most violent impact event in recorded history.

27
00:02:16,318 --> 00:02:21,366
And almost nobody outside Siberia knew exactly where it had happened.

28
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:25,085
Then the file sat closed for thirteen years.

29
00:02:25,085 --> 00:02:28,115
War, revolution and civil war came first.

30
00:02:28,115 --> 00:02:33,733
The first scientific expedition would not set out until nineteen twenty-one.

31
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:36,437
Section two: witness statements.

32
00:02:36,437 --> 00:02:43,499
More than seven hundred accounts were collected between nineteen-oh-eight and the nineteen-seventies.

33
00:02:43,499 --> 00:02:49,233
The most famous comes from Semyon Semyonov, a farmer at the Vanavara trading post.

34
00:02:49,699 --> 00:03:00,931
He was sitting on the porch at breakfast, about to hoop a barrel, when, in his words, the sky was split in two, and the whole northern part of the sky seemed covered with fire.

35
00:03:00,931 --> 00:03:03,866
He felt heat, as if his shirt had caught fire.

36
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:11,358
Then a bang, a mighty crash, and he was thrown about six metres from the porch and lost consciousness.

37
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:14,251
Window panes at the trading post blew out.

38
00:03:14,251 --> 00:03:16,800
The iron hasp on the barn door broke.

39
00:03:17,266 --> 00:03:21,842
Closer in, an Evenki man called Chuchan was asleep in his tent.

40
00:03:21,842 --> 00:03:31,866
He described being thrown into the hearth, trees falling, needles and reindeer moss burning, and heat, as he put it, enough to burn alive.

41
00:03:32,333 --> 00:03:39,117
Notice what these statements describe: an object moving across the sky, then a blast in the air.

42
00:03:39,117 --> 00:03:40,884
Not a light that hovered.

43
00:03:40,884 --> 00:03:42,580
Not a craft that turned.

44
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:44,700
A fireball on a straight line.

45
00:03:45,166 --> 00:03:49,370
Some witnesses thought another planet had struck the Earth.

46
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:54,002
Many Evenki saw the anger of Ogdy, their god of fire and thunder.

47
00:03:54,002 --> 00:03:57,992
Every age reads a sky like that through its own beliefs.

48
00:03:57,992 --> 00:04:00,699
Ours reads it through science fiction.

49
00:04:01,166 --> 00:04:05,032
Memory fades, and the later accounts are less reliable.

50
00:04:05,032 --> 00:04:10,866
But the core never changes: a fireball, a wave of heat, and a blast from the north.

51
00:04:11,333 --> 00:04:13,711
Section three: the site survey.

52
00:04:13,711 --> 00:04:21,841
In nineteen twenty-seven, the mineralogist Leonid Kulik finally reached the blast zone with Evenki guides.

53
00:04:21,841 --> 00:04:26,366
He expected a giant crater, and a fortune in meteoric iron.

54
00:04:26,833 --> 00:04:28,119
There was no crater.

55
00:04:28,119 --> 00:04:34,679
At the centre, trees were still standing, upright, but stripped of every branch, like telegraph poles.

56
00:04:34,679 --> 00:04:39,566
Farther out, the forest lay flat, every trunk pointing away from the centre.

57
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:50,600
Later surveys mapped the fallen zone at about two thousand one hundred and fifty square kilometres, shaped like a butterfly with a wingspan of seventy kilometres.

58
00:04:51,066 --> 00:04:55,095
Kulik believed some boggy holes were meteorite craters.

59
00:04:55,095 --> 00:04:59,711
He drained one, called Suslov's crater, thirty-two metres wide.

60
00:04:59,711 --> 00:05:02,715
At the bottom he found an old tree stump.

61
00:05:02,715 --> 00:05:04,399
Not a crater after all.

62
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:13,260
In nineteen thirty-eight he arranged an aerial survey: fifteen hundred photographic negatives of the central zone.

63
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:19,666
The original negatives were burned in nineteen seventy-five, as flammable nitrate film.

64
00:05:19,666 --> 00:05:21,433
Prints survive in Tomsk.

65
00:05:21,899 --> 00:05:23,874
Kulik never closed the file.

66
00:05:23,874 --> 00:05:32,899
In the Second World War he joined the militia, was captured, and died of typhus in a prisoner-of-war camp in nineteen forty-two.

67
00:05:33,366 --> 00:05:35,457
Here is the key forensic clue.

68
00:05:35,457 --> 00:05:42,007
Standing trees in the middle, flattened trees in a ring: that is what a blast from above does.

69
00:05:42,007 --> 00:05:51,066
In the nineteen-sixties, Soviet scientists rebuilt it with model forests of matches and small charges, and got the same butterfly.

70
00:05:51,533 --> 00:05:56,665
The blast came from the air, several kilometres up, angled in from the south-east.

71
00:05:56,665 --> 00:06:00,233
So whatever it was never touched the ground in one piece.

72
00:06:00,699 --> 00:06:02,606
Section four: the wreckage.

73
00:06:02,606 --> 00:06:05,996
A crash investigator's first question is simple.

74
00:06:05,996 --> 00:06:07,479
Where are the pieces?

75
00:06:07,479 --> 00:06:12,000
Expeditions in the nineteen-fifties and sixties sifted the soil.

76
00:06:12,466 --> 00:06:21,494
They found microscopic silicate and magnetite spheres, rich in nickel compared with iron, the kind of ratio found in meteorites.

77
00:06:21,494 --> 00:06:25,866
And their spread in the soil matched debris from an air burst.

78
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:37,779
In the nineteen-nineties, Italian researchers led by Giuseppe Longo of the University of Bologna extracted resin from trees that lived through the blast.

79
00:06:37,779 --> 00:06:43,166
Trapped inside: material typical of rocky asteroids, and rare in comets.

80
00:06:43,633 --> 00:06:51,948
Peat bogs hold a nineteen-oh-eight layer with odd carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen isotopes, and reported iridium.

81
00:06:51,948 --> 00:06:57,266
To be fair, other laboratories have not confirmed all of those results.

82
00:06:57,733 --> 00:07:06,266
And the boulder known as John's stone, once hoped to be a piece of the impactor, turned out to be ordinary Earth rock, a quartzite.

83
00:07:06,733 --> 00:07:11,277
Then there is Lake Cheko, about eight kilometres from the epicentre.

84
00:07:11,277 --> 00:07:20,966
In two thousand and seven, a Bologna team proposed it was a crater dug by a ten-metre fragment, citing its funnel-shaped bottom and its sediment.

85
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:31,363
In twenty seventeen, Russian scientists counted at least two hundred and eighty yearly layers in the lake bed, which would make it older than the blast.

86
00:07:31,363 --> 00:07:32,866
The argument continues.

87
00:07:33,333 --> 00:07:35,134
So there is no big wreck.

88
00:07:35,134 --> 00:07:35,854
Just dust.

89
00:07:35,854 --> 00:07:44,066
To a crash investigator, that is a clue in itself: something blew apart so completely that nothing large was left.

90
00:07:44,533 --> 00:07:47,677
And notice what is not on this wreckage list.

91
00:07:47,677 --> 00:07:48,306
No alloy.

92
00:07:48,306 --> 00:07:49,284
No hull plate.

93
00:07:49,284 --> 00:07:50,332
No engine part.

94
00:07:50,332 --> 00:07:53,266
Not from more than a century of searching.

95
00:07:53,733 --> 00:07:55,743
Section five: the flight path.

96
00:07:55,743 --> 00:08:00,971
Cross-checking witnesses, the tree-fall and computer models gives a direction.

97
00:08:00,971 --> 00:08:06,533
The body came in from the south-east, at a shallow angle of roughly thirty degrees.

98
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,976
It exploded somewhere between five and ten kilometres up.

99
00:08:10,976 --> 00:08:18,372
Models suggest a body around fifty to sixty metres across, moving at more than eleven kilometres a second.

100
00:08:18,372 --> 00:08:24,233
Energy estimates run from about three to thirty megatons, most often ten to fifteen.

101
00:08:24,699 --> 00:08:26,473
Now, comet or asteroid?

102
00:08:26,473 --> 00:08:28,556
In nineteen thirty-four, F.

103
00:08:28,556 --> 00:08:28,710
J.

104
00:08:28,710 --> 00:08:28,864
W.

105
00:08:28,864 --> 00:08:35,729
Whipple proposed a small comet: ice and dust that would vaporise and leave little behind.

106
00:08:35,729 --> 00:08:39,199
It might explain the glowing night skies too.

107
00:08:39,666 --> 00:08:50,100
In nineteen seventy-eight, Lubor Kresák linked it to Comet Encke, parent of the Beta Taurid meteor shower, which peaks at the very end of June.

108
00:08:50,566 --> 00:08:59,436
But in nineteen eighty-three, Zdeněk Sekanina argued that a fragile comet on such a shallow path should have broken up higher.

109
00:08:59,436 --> 00:09:01,971
A dense, stony asteroid fits better.

110
00:09:01,971 --> 00:09:09,433
A two thousand and one orbit study put the odds at eighty-three percent asteroid, seventeen percent comet.

111
00:09:09,899 --> 00:09:18,820
And in nineteen ninety-three, Christopher Chyba and colleagues showed how a stony asteroid can blow itself apart in mid-air.

112
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:25,151
Once the air pressure outstrips its strength, it releases almost all its energy at once.

113
00:09:25,151 --> 00:09:25,871
No crater.

114
00:09:25,871 --> 00:09:26,734
Wide damage.

115
00:09:26,734 --> 00:09:28,533
Exactly what Kulik found.

116
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:37,059
There is even a twenty twenty model of an iron asteroid that grazed the atmosphere and flew back out into space.

117
00:09:37,059 --> 00:09:39,794
One flight path, several explanations.

118
00:09:39,794 --> 00:09:41,233
All of them natural.

119
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:43,874
Section six: the recorders.

120
00:09:43,874 --> 00:09:46,209
Air crashes have black boxes.

121
00:09:46,209 --> 00:09:50,558
Tunguska had instruments thousands of kilometres away.

122
00:09:50,558 --> 00:09:57,966
Seismographs at Irkutsk, Tashkent, Tiflis and Jena recorded a shock close to magnitude five.

123
00:09:58,433 --> 00:10:08,033
Barographs in Britain and Germany, and as far away as Washington and Batavia, logged the air-pressure wave as it rolled around the globe.

124
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:16,484
And for several nights, skies over western Eurasia glowed so brightly that people could read a newspaper at midnight.

125
00:10:16,484 --> 00:10:21,466
In England, astronomers gave up observing because the sky was too bright.

126
00:10:21,933 --> 00:10:32,399
Measurements at Mount Wilson in California later suggested a drop in the clarity of the air, consistent with fine dust spread through the upper atmosphere.

127
00:10:32,866 --> 00:10:36,501
One recorder matters most for the alien file.

128
00:10:36,501 --> 00:10:39,813
A nuclear explosion leaves radioactivity.

129
00:10:39,813 --> 00:10:48,700
So, in nineteen fifty-nine, young volunteers from Tomsk, led by Gennady Plekhanov, walked in with radiometers.

130
00:10:49,166 --> 00:10:51,548
Their first readings looked raised.

131
00:10:51,548 --> 00:10:57,604
But they found no engine nozzles, and no radioactivity clearly tied to nineteen-oh-eight.

132
00:10:57,604 --> 00:11:03,933
Radioactivity in the trees was traced to fallout from the bomb tests of the nineteen-fifties.

133
00:11:04,399 --> 00:11:06,549
Section seven: the suspects.

134
00:11:06,549 --> 00:11:08,161
Start with the craft.

135
00:11:08,161 --> 00:11:18,600
The spaceship idea has a birthday: January nineteen forty-six, a short story called Explosion, by the Soviet writer Alexander Kazantsev.

136
00:11:19,066 --> 00:11:21,648
Kazantsev had read about Hiroshima.

137
00:11:21,648 --> 00:11:26,886
He imagined a nuclear-powered ship from Mars blowing up over the taiga.

138
00:11:26,886 --> 00:11:27,992
It was fiction.

139
00:11:27,992 --> 00:11:32,566
But it was so vivid that many readers took it as a hypothesis.

140
00:11:33,033 --> 00:11:40,321
To be fair to him, Kazantsev saw something real: an explosion in the air explains the missing crater.

141
00:11:40,321 --> 00:11:44,579
But a natural air burst explains it too, without radiation.

142
00:11:44,579 --> 00:11:47,033
And the radiation was never there.

143
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:59,612
In two thousand and four, Yuri Lavbin of a Siberian foundation called Tunguska Space Phenomenon announced blocks of an extraterrestrial device near the village of Poligus.

144
00:11:59,612 --> 00:12:04,500
He had said in advance that he intended to prove a spaceship crashed.

145
00:12:04,966 --> 00:12:13,579
The space writer James Oberg pointed out that the region lies under drop zones for spent rocket stages launched from Baikonur.

146
00:12:13,579 --> 00:12:17,133
We could find no peer-reviewed analysis of the find.

147
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:18,650
Other suspects.

148
00:12:18,650 --> 00:12:22,572
Nikola Tesla's death ray, fired from Wardenclyffe Tower?

149
00:12:22,572 --> 00:12:28,034
That story was invented in a fictionalised biography in the nineteen-nineties.

150
00:12:28,034 --> 00:12:33,566
Tesla never finished the tower, and wrote of no such beam in nineteen-oh-eight.

151
00:12:34,033 --> 00:12:36,580
Physicists tried exotic ideas too.

152
00:12:36,580 --> 00:12:40,025
In nineteen sixty-five, a chunk of antimatter.

153
00:12:40,025 --> 00:12:45,343
In nineteen seventy-three, a tiny black hole passing through the Earth.

154
00:12:45,343 --> 00:12:53,433
Neither left the signature it predicts: no confirmed radiocarbon spike, no exit blast in the North Atlantic.

155
00:12:53,899 --> 00:12:58,783
And a few geophysicists proposed natural gas erupting from the ground.

156
00:12:58,783 --> 00:13:02,899
But nearly every witness saw something cross the sky first.

157
00:13:03,366 --> 00:13:07,052
Then, in February twenty thirteen, nature ran a test.

158
00:13:07,052 --> 00:13:14,006
An asteroid about eighteen metres wide exploded some thirty kilometres above Chelyabinsk, in Russia.

159
00:13:14,006 --> 00:13:16,510
About four to five hundred kilotons.

160
00:13:16,510 --> 00:13:20,613
Around fifteen hundred people hurt, mostly by flying glass.

161
00:13:20,613 --> 00:13:22,699
And meteorites were recovered.

162
00:13:23,166 --> 00:13:25,100
Section eight: probable cause.

163
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:26,454
The file is complete.

164
00:13:26,454 --> 00:13:28,066
Let's score the findings.

165
00:13:28,533 --> 00:13:32,637
A fireball crossing the sky, seen by hundreds: evidence.

166
00:13:32,637 --> 00:13:37,987
Standing trees ringed by a flattened butterfly: evidence of an air burst.

167
00:13:37,987 --> 00:13:44,143
Nickel-rich spheres and asteroid-type particles in resin: evidence of a cosmic body.

168
00:13:44,143 --> 00:13:49,566
A stony asteroid some fifty to sixty metres wide: the most likely culprit.

169
00:13:50,033 --> 00:13:54,940
A nuclear alien spaceship: no evidence, and no radioactivity.

170
00:13:54,940 --> 00:14:00,410
The two thousand and four alien device: unverified, never published.

171
00:14:00,410 --> 00:14:04,433
Asteroid versus comet, and Lake Cheko: still open.

172
00:14:04,899 --> 00:14:16,146
Probable cause: a natural object from space, most likely a stony asteroid, exploded five to ten kilometres above the taiga with the force of a large nuclear weapon.

173
00:14:16,146 --> 00:14:21,633
There was no crash, because nothing big enough to crash ever reached the ground.

174
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:26,430
The craft entered this file in nineteen forty-six, as a story.

175
00:14:26,430 --> 00:14:32,366
Every test that could have found it, radioactivity, wreckage, metal, came back empty.

176
00:14:32,833 --> 00:14:36,230
Every accident report ends with recommendations.

177
00:14:36,230 --> 00:14:38,070
This one already has them.

178
00:14:38,070 --> 00:14:47,766
Since twenty sixteen, the thirtieth of June has been the United Nations' International Asteroid Day, a reminder to keep watching the sky.

179
00:14:48,233 --> 00:14:56,459
Blasts the size of Tunguska are thought to happen roughly once every few centuries, perhaps once in a thousand years.

180
00:14:56,459 --> 00:14:59,272
This one fell on an almost empty forest.

181
00:14:59,272 --> 00:15:02,858
The real mystery of Tunguska is not who was flying.

182
00:15:02,858 --> 00:15:06,233
It is whether, next time, we will see it coming.

183
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,407
Comet, asteroid, or craft?

184
00:15:08,407 --> 00:15:13,333
Give us your verdict in the comments, and subscribe for the next case file.
